# Existence of a separating transcendental element on a curve

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver.exists_separating_transcendental`
(`532c00b1-0629-5b3f-9739-d3d27cbcbf36`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `f6204aaa-e070-50ce-9afd-cc4c321cff10`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, $K$ perfect, and $F$ essentially of finite type
over $K$, and assume $F$ is a curve over $K$ in the sense of the predicate `IsCurveOver`: namely (i)
$F/K$ has principal divisors, in that for every $f \in F$ with $f \neq 0$ there is a divisor $D$ on
$F/K$ with $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f)$ at every place $v$ and $\deg D = 0$, where a place is a
valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, distinct from $F$ itself, and a principal
ideal ring; (ii) for every such place $v$ the residue field of the corresponding valuation subring
is a finite $K$-module; and (iii) the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of rank
one over $F$. Then there exists $t \in F$ which is transcendental over $K$ and such that $F$ is
finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K\langle t\rangle$ generated by $t$ and is separable
over $K\langle t\rangle$; that is, $F/K(t)$ is a finite separable extension.

This is the existence of a separating element (equivalently, a separating transcendence basis of
size one) for a curve over a perfect field, the direction of the standard characterisation of
algebraic function fields in one variable that produces a rational subfield $K(t)$ over which $F$ is
finite separable. It supplies the $K(t)$-tower presentation used throughout the development, and is
invoked by the results on divisors, genus, residues and component charts built on `IsCurveOver`.

